I suspect the global rank of the areas a language is spread to plays a
major role: the african countries in which french is the common
language wield a lot less power than, say, Australia.

The dominance of english is somewhat different from the previous
european "world languages" french and latin, in that it dominates by
having a very broad base of speakers, while french and (in the middle
ages) latin were adequately spoken by relatively few but influential
non-natives - not that there was a population of true native speakers
of latin at the time.

